<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trans-Amazonian Highway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1972</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as BR-230</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trans-Amazonian Highway, BR-230, is a road across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> launched by the military government in 1970 to open the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> to settlement. Running some 4,000 kilometres from the Atlantic coast towards the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, it was intended to settle landless farmers from the north-east, but much of it remains unpaved and it led to large-scale <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">deforestation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trans-amazonian-highway.jpg" alt="Trans-Amazonian Highway" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vitalino Pimenta · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Br-230mapa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
